# Around the Markoff uniqueness conjecture: manuscripts and reproduction
# material.
#
# Authors:
#   Dr. Denys Dutykh (Mathematics Department, Khalifa University of Science
#   and Technology, Abu Dhabi, UAE)
#   Prof. Laurent Vuillon (Univ. Savoie Mont Blanc, CNRS, LAMA, Chambery,
#   France)
#
# Citation metadata in Citation File Format 1.2.0. GitHub renders the
# "Cite this repository" button from this file. It describes the repository as
# a whole; each manuscript below papers/ is cited on its own, and its README
# gives the entry for it.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite the repository as below, and the individual manuscript you rely on."
title: "Around the Markoff uniqueness conjecture: manuscripts and reproduction material"
abstract: >-
  Manuscript sources and reproduction supplements for a series of papers on the
  Markoff-Frobenius uniqueness conjecture, which asks whether a Markoff number
  determines its Markoff triple. Each manuscript settles one face of the
  question completely on an explicitly stated domain: analytic estimates reduce
  an unbounded problem to a finite one, and an exact computation in integer or
  rational arithmetic, reproducible from the supplement that travels with the
  manuscript, finishes it. None of them claims the conjecture. The first
  manuscript classifies the strict factor-two Fibonacci-Pell nearest gaps whose
  quadratic norm equals minus the square of their coefficient content, finds
  exactly two and none with an odd exponent, and lists every Pell-orbit
  intersection of the two signed Fibonacci cores. The second proves a stability
  theorem for Euclidean costs along a finite weighted graph of
  continued-fraction states and applies it to the Gaussian reflection attached
  to 89, showing that every distinct reflected target of a genuine reduced
  Markoff source has strictly larger cost. The third gives, for every fixed
  occurrence count, a complete decision procedure for equal traces of simple
  closed geodesics on the modular torus, with an exact isometry test, and shows
  that all such equalities are isometric for occurrence counts two to six and
  for intersection numbers at most twenty-four.
